[ ] Export sandbox telemetry with standard attributes =sandbox-telemetry $Sessions export OTLP to the NUC collector, which writes agent-logs, agent-metrics and agent-traces jsonl under ~/.local/state/otelcol. From a container, the collector answers on the tailscale address, not host.containers.internal. Attributes, standard only: service.name and service.version from the harness itself, host.name, deployment.environment.name sandbox or interactive, vcs.repository.name, vcs.ref.head.name, vcs.ref.head.revision, cicd.pipeline.name agent-sandbox, cicd.pipeline.run.id for the run and cicd.pipeline.task.run.id per session. No clearhead attributes: runs join to actions through commits. The driver also timestamps build and each session in the manifest. Write the convention, with this table and its reasons, to docs/telemetry.md; interactive harness configs adopt host.name and deployment.environment.name. Sequence after the run-kind/driver boundary and automatic reconciliation stabilize: export meaningful terminal outcomes and systemd resource events, rather than instrumenting the current mixed-policy launcher twice. 2026-09-30: this is the observability half of the review feedback loop; the other half is the review and human-verdict records in the manifests, joined by run id.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a
[ ] Query sandbox runs with duckdb =sandbox-benchmarks $A saved duckdb query file over ~/agent-runs manifests and the collector jsonl, answering: cost and time per outcome; build time against agent time; tool calls and gate runs per session; sandbox against interactive sessions. Questions first, no dashboard. Do after sandbox-telemetry. ADDED 2026-09-30: calibration questions from the review feedback loop: per reviewer model and kind of change, the miss rate (human 'missed' entries) and false-alarm rate (overruled findings), and whether transcript coverage predicts misses.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 <01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a #01a0dab3-2df6-7862-8b3a-df005de98823
[ ] Extract the sandbox into its own repo =sandbox-extract $Decided 2026-09-25 after evaluate-agent-runners: we write our own. Do after sandbox-driver-split, once the runner, harness adapters, harvest and land contain no ClearHead reference; grep for it as the check. The ClearHead driver stays on the ClearHead side. Extraction surfaces the remaining platform assumptions: the agents directory mount, the host pi paths, agent branch naming, and harvest assuming submodules. Proof of done: a run, harvest and land on a second repo that has no .clearhead directory.  ADDED 2026-09-29: harness tooling must leave the target repo's image. Today the platform Containerfile installs claude-code and pi, which a general runner cannot require of every repo; the runner adds its own harness layer over the repo's toolchain image, and owns the harness version pins there. The pending pi bump belongs to that layer: pi 0.99.1 (released 2026-09-29) has gpt-6.1-sol under the same openai-codex provider id, but 0.85.1 to 0.99.1 spans breaking changes, so bump it with a smoke run.  PREPARED 2026-09-30 after sandbox-driver-split: the runner no longer mentions ClearHead (grep leaves only two readers of pre-split records in agent-status and agent-harvest). NEEDS the human first on two points, ask before coding: port to Rust or Babashka or move the POSIX sh as is (about 900 lines, working); and the repo's name and whether platform uses it as a submodule or from PATH. Runner files to move: scripts/agent-new, agent-run, agent-result, agent-reconcile, agent-stop, agent-status, agent-harvest, agent-land, scripts/lib/agent-quadlet.sh and agent-runs.sh, both test files, agents/session, agents/models.env. Stays in platform: scripts/clearhead-work, .sandbox/, docs/overnight-runbook.md. Platform assumptions still in the runner, from reading the code: 1) every script finds the target repo as the parent of its own directory; it must become the git toplevel of the caller's cwd. 2) agent-new snapshots the target repo's agents/ directory; session and models.env must come from the tool's own install. 3) the root Containerfile installs claude-code and pi; split it into the repo's toolchain image and a harness layer the runner builds on top. 4) agent-run mounts Rust-only cache volumes agent-cargo and agent-target, and the image sets CARGO_TARGET_DIR; a repo should declare its own cache volumes under .sandbox. 5) agent-new reads the host's pi settings unconditionally, so a host without pi cannot create a workspace. 6) agent-land defaults its gate to scripts/validate-pinned and has submodule pin logic; the gate should come from the repo, and landing a repo with no submodules is unverified. 7) agent-harvest labels the root repo platform. 8) limits are fixed at 8 CPUs and 16G in agent-run and the unit renderer. Proof of done stays: a session, harvest and land on a second repo with no .clearhead directory. DECIDED 2026-09-30 with the human: move the POSIX sh as is, no port (revisit only when the sh hurts). It lives in a sibling repo of platform (~/Products/<name>), used from PATH, not as a submodule. Name: the human suggested agent-jobs; settle it when the repo is created. Sequence: after sandbox-premerge-gate and one real multi-action work run with a cross-vendor review, so the interface is exercised before it is frozen. Name settled 2026-09-30: agent-sandbox, at ~/Products/agent-sandbox. NOTE 2026-10-02: the NUC is being moved to an immutable container OS, and this desktop cannot host runs (no podman, 15 GB RAM). Bringing the NUC back up is the first portability test: log every host setup break (e.g. assumption 5, agent-new reading ~/.pi settings) as an extraction finding. Install target is user-space (~/.local/bin or similar); nothing installs into the read-only host. Planned split: in-platform sessions remove the 8 assumptions (one workspace, the stop-classification fix as session 1, 2-3 sessions grouped by file, cross-vendor review); then the human moves the files (subtree split or filter-repo, keeping history) and does the proof run on open-phealth. The sandbox cannot do the move: its only output is commits to its platform clone.  PROGRESS 2026-10-03, by hand on the desktop (podman now installed): the runner lives in platform/agent-sandbox/{bin,lib,agents,test} with its own README, the future repo's layout, used from PATH. Assumptions 1 (tools find their install as tool; agent-new clones the caller's git toplevel and records it as manifest .repo, which harvest and land read), 2 (agents/ snapshot from the install), 4 (cache volumes from the branch's .sandbox/volumes, named volumes only, prefixed agent-cache-), 5 (pi settings and volumes only when present and only for pi sessions), 6 (landing without submodules, tested), 7 (harvest names the repo) and 8 (AGENT_CPUS, AGENT_MEMORY, the host's) are done; clearhead-work uses the sandbox from PATH. Host finding: a pi settings.json without a packages key failed agent-new; fixed. Verified: all test/*.test.sh including land; the platform image builds here; a session through systemd on a throwaway second repo without .clearhead mounted its cache volume and sourced its setup (the session itself failed at pi login, as expected). Then, with the claude_token secret on this host: a Claude session (sonnet-5-5, 0.03 USD) on a throwaway repo without .clearhead committed a file, and agent-harvest and agent-land, run from outside that repo, landed it through its own .sandbox/gate. That repo's Containerfile was FROM platform-agent, so it does not test 3. Then 3, decided with the human 2026-10-03 (option A: the repo builds its image as root on any base and must provide git, jq and npm; the sandbox owns the harnesses, their pins and the uid-1000 user in agents/Containerfile, built FROM the repo's image by build_agent_image): platform's Containerfile lost its harness and user section; a throwaway repo on debian:trixie-slim with only git, jq and npm ran a Claude session, harvest and land, the commit owned by the host user. EXTRACTED 2026-10-03: git filter-repo moved the runner with its history (37 commits, old scripts/ paths mapped onto bin/, lib/, test/) to ~/Products/agent-sandbox, and this charter moved with it as the repo's root charter (platform keeps scripts/clearhead-work, .sandbox/ and the runbook, and calls the tools from PATH). INSTALL 2026-10-03, decided with the human: no install step. An install script was tried and dropped as imperative (it made undeclared state); installing is a declaration kept by whatever declares the host (this repo at a pinned revision, its bin/ on PATH, e.g. a chezmoi external plus a PATH line). The tools resolve their install through symlinks (readlink -f). agent-doctor is the read-only host check (git, jq, flock, systemctl, podman 4.4+ for Quadlet, uid 1000, subuid and subgid; notes for the claude_token secret, a non-empty pi auth.json in the agent-pi volume, lingering). Found on the way: agent-status exited 1 with no workspaces (fixed); an empty pi auth.json left by a failed session (the doctor does not count it as a login). Deployment split agreed with the human: the personal-os image provides the host prerequisites (podman is not in mini-server yet; that agent has it), the sandbox stays a user-space checkout, credentials are per host. PUBLISHED 2026-10-03 at https://github.com/ca-mantis-shrimp/agent-sandbox (public, MIT as platform). LEFT: tags so a declaration can pin a version; the proof on open-phealth, which needs its own Containerfile (Rust plus git, jq, npm) and .sandbox/; give this repo a remote. Cache volume names changed (agent-cargo, agent-target become agent-cache-cargo, agent-cache-target), so the first session on an existing host rebuilds its caches.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T16:13 <01a0dab3-2d95-7d53-a65e-cb88d090e1bd <01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf #01a0dad8-d9ba-79f1-8a9f-b5b68c0e3b0a
[ ] Automatically reconcile detached Quadlet runs =sandbox-auto-reconcile $Follow-up to landed run 20260925-215528. On the host, terminating the foreground systemctl start --wait caller left the Quadlet active with Result=success and ExecMainCode=0. The landed launcher correctly preserves the active unit and manifest; scripts/agent-status flags RECONCILE after it stops and scripts/agent-reconcile <id> is safe, idempotent manual recovery. For unattended operation, make terminal reconciliation automatic without a shell polling supervisor: use a systemd-owned completion/reaper mechanism or another bounded durable trigger. Preserve Git-only output, run isolation, no automatic mutation retry, and the explicit manual command. Acceptance: interrupt the launcher during a bounded no-model unit; the unit continues; when it exits the manifest reaches the correct terminal outcome and unit file is cleaned without operator intervention. Prove success, failure, timeout, OOM and manual stop retain accurate status; record recovery evidence in the manifest. Do not conflate this with the separate interactive model-control-channel design. Sequence first for unattended safety: systemd can outlive the launcher, but terminal manifest cleanup must not depend on an operator remembering the manual command. Preserve agent-reconcile as recovery and test a killed launcher against a real bounded unit.  NOTE 2026-09-30, after sandbox-driver-split, re-read before working: launchers no longer wait by default, and a stopped session is now finalized lazily by whoever touches the workspace next: scripts/agent-result, the next scripts/agent-run --in, and scripts/agent-harvest all call agent-reconcile, which works per session (unit agent-WORKSPACE-N, record sessions/N.json). What is left of this action is reconciliation when nobody calls anything, the systemd-owned trigger. Rewrite the acceptance steps for sessions: the unit and record names above replace the per-run ones.$ !1 ^2026-09-25T22:40 #01a0dc3a-f71b-7f82-97be-829c00623a8e
[=] Define a read-only planner and proposal handoff =sandbox-planner-contract $NEEDS DECISION: who approves and applies planner-proposed ClearHead action/charter changes—the human, a deliberating council, or a bounded driver policy? A genuinely read-only planner cannot directly mutate .clearhead; have it inspect the action, charter, specs, code and prior run evidence, validate feasibility, and emit a structured proposal with candidate CLI mutations, acceptance checks, dependencies, unknowns and stop/ask points. A distinct authorized materializer applies accepted changes through the ClearHead CLI and commits them; proposals are evidence, not authority. Alternative: grant narrowly scoped .clearhead write access in an isolated clone, but then this is a writer role, not read-only. Keep this ClearHead-specific policy in the driver, while the sandbox only hosts a generic read-only run kind and artifact. Preserve human authority over consequential decisions and avoid making a planner the top-down dispatcher. Decide the approval boundary before implementing.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:45 #01a0dc3f-bb67-76d2-a4ad-920ae1f90b3a
[=] Design interruptible interactive agent sessions =sandbox-interactive-control $NEEDS DECISION: what authenticated local input/control channel may interrupt an active sandbox model session without weakening the Git-only code-output boundary? Quadlet attachment alone cannot add a turn to current headless pi -p/Claude -p sessions. Specify cancellation versus checkpoint/resume, transport (persistent RPC or PTY relay), audit of human interventions, unit/manifest identity, credential isolation, failure/reconnect behavior, and whether a controller may write only prompts or broader tools. Preserve resource caps and independent review. This is a separate mode from unattended one-turn work; do not change the active runner until the authority and security contract is approved.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:45 #01a0dc3f-bb8e-7192-8113-f2b84efa412e
[ ] Pilot a lower-cost worker model on decided work =sandbox-cheap-worker-pilot $After the planner proposal contract and first-class cross-vendor review run exist, pilot a cheaper worker route such as DeepSeek via the sandbox harness adapter on a few bounded, already-decided actions in isolated Quadlets. Strong planning and independent review are advisory gates, not automatic authority; no self-review. Keep the same spend, wall-time and commit/gate contracts. Compare cost and elapsed time per accepted landed action, retries, stop/NEEDS DECISION rate and reviewer effort against a stronger worker baseline; cheap tokens alone are not the success metric. Record model/provider per session and let the driver select roles by policy without hard-coding ClearHead or a model into the generic sandbox. Do not make the cheap model the default for broad multi-seam or high-risk tasks without evidence.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:46 <01a0dc3f-bb67-76d2-a4ad-920ae1f90b3a <01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf #01a0dc3f-e6ce-7cd3-802a-473ab21aa73a
[ ] Classify a requested stop as stopped, not failed $Found 2026-09-30, the first real agent-stop under per-session units: stopping a running Claude session (workspace 20260930-133222, session 1) recorded outcome failed:exit:137, not stopped. classify_agent_outcome maps stop_requested=1 with Result=exit-code to failed:exit:<status>; a container killed after the stop request exits 137 (128+SIGKILL), so a requested stop reads as a failure. Decide which exit statuses after a requested stop count as stopped (143 SIGTERM, 137 SIGKILL at least), add the cases to scripts/agent-quadlet.test.sh, and check the smoke on a real session.$ !2 ^2026-09-30T14:29 #01a0f438-a648-76d2-8c6e-53cc613ad73b
[ ] Prove an unattended run on the homelab =sandbox-unattended $Overnight runs belong on the homelab, which stays on; the desktop is turned off and on (the human, 2026-10-03). A session is already a systemd unit; the driver that starts sessions must run under the user manager too (systemd-run --user or a user timer), not in a terminal. README, Unattended runs, says what the host needs; agent-doctor checks lingering and whether the user manager's PATH finds agent-run. Host side, handed to the personal-os agent: lingering for the account, an environment.d PATH entry from dotfiles (the line in the README was checked with systemd's environment-d generator), and no suspend. Proof: on the homelab, a driver started with systemd-run --user runs two sessions while nobody is logged in, and agent-result shows both finished afterwards. Which queue runs overnight and on what schedule is the project driver's decision (platform's clearhead-work), not the sandbox's.$ !2 ^2026-10-03T11:39 #01a10310-b607-70b0-88fb-1ce5c78dfa5b
[ ] Fix the pi login command in the README's host setup $The documented command (podman run --rm -it --userns=keep-id -v agent-pi:/home/agent/.pi/agent localhost/<repo>-agent pi, then /login) fails at the last step for the openai-codex provider. The browser's OAuth redirect to localhost reaches the host, not the container, which publishes no ports. Adding --network=host worked on 2026-10-08. Alternative: publish only the callback port, if pi binds it on all interfaces.$ +bug ^2026-10-08T10:05 #01a11c7a-1513-707f-ae0b-9c1242f0c798
[ ] Make the pi provider configurable and check its login fits $DECIDED 2026-10-08 (Darrion): take the recommendation below, a provider setting plus a doctor check that its login exists; no injected host auth.json. Context: agents/session hard-coded --provider openai-codex, which pi 0.99.2 labels legacy; /login stored the token under openai, so the session failed at \$0 (run 20261008-100519). Fixed in 3fc7394 by hard-coding openai instead, so the next rename breaks it again. agent-doctor passed throughout because it only checks that auth.json is non-empty. Recommendation: AGENT_PI_PROVIDER in agents/models.env next to AGENT_PI_MODEL (same override rules), passed to --provider, and agent-doctor checks that auth.json has a key for that provider. Fold in the README login bug (--network=host) at the same time. Considered 2026-10-08: inject the host's auth.json, since pi holds several logins. Against it: OAuth refresh tokens rotate, so a shared login means whichever side refreshes first logs the other out, and a read-only mount can't refresh at all. It would also hand every provider's credentials to every session (the README already says never the host's auth.json). The agent-pi volume can hold several logins too; what is missing is choosing the provider per session and checking that its login exists. API-key providers could use one podman secret each, like claude_token.$ ^2026-10-08T10:10 #01a11c7e-7882-705f-aa01-05b735f9a6b2
[ ] Retire a workspace that produced no work $A failed or empty run (e.g. 20261008-100519: startup failure, no commits) has no way out except landing. Proposed: agent-retire <workspace> refuses if a session is running or any repo has commits beyond refs/agent/base, then removes the clone and any candidate and keeps sessions, prompts and transcripts, like a successful landing does. It records the retirement in the manifest so agent-status stops listing it as open.$ ^2026-10-08T10:10 #01a11c7e-7895-7410-b077-9da607f8962e
[ ] Let sessions raise a hand\: question files, agent-answer, and waiting questions in agent-status $Idea (Darrion and the assistant, 2026-10-08); not scheduled. Today a stuck session's only hand is the stop rule: blocked plus NEEDS DECISION, then a restart. Decided 2026-10-08 (Darrion): this is a sandbox feature, and no separate inbox tool. Shape: (1) a session writes a question file into its workspace dir (/job/<ws>/questions/<n>.md, outside work/, so git output stays clean) and blocks on one helper call that waits for an answer file or a timeout. On timeout it falls back to the normal NEEDS DECISION stop, so a question that outlives the session lands in the project's own task system, not here. One call, not a polling loop, so waiting costs no tokens. (2) agent-status shows waiting questions: a count per workspace and the question's first line, plus a quick check for 'anything to answer?' (exits non-zero when any are waiting). (3) agent-answer <ws>/<q> <text|file> writes the answer. Who answers: whoever launched the session. An orchestrator answers context questions and escalates design ones to the human. Narrower than sandbox-interactive-control: the agent initiates, plain files, nothing new can get in except text the agent reads. Costs to weigh: a waiting session holds its container, writer lock and deadline clock, and cheap asking can replace deciding (a question mid-task often means the action was under-decided). Evidence to watch first: stops whose answer was one line.$ !5 ^2026-10-08T10:21 #01a11c88-c566-7139-be11-4b8486f9c373
[ ] Evaluate VM isolation with systemd-vmspawn instead of Podman $Question, not scheduled (Darrion and the assistant, 2026-10-08). Agents run arbitrary code; rootless containers share the host kernel and a VM does not. That matters most on a host where the orchestrator runs as root, where mkosi and vmspawn are the native tools. Cost of switching: replacing what Podman provides today (rootless keep-id so commits stay the user's, Quadlet units, named cache volumes, secrets) and changing the project contract from a root Containerfile to an mkosi config in every repository. Earlier attempt: a cwork script using sudo systemd-nspawn; needing root is probably why Podman won. No failure so far has come from Podman. First, a cheap probe: can an unprivileged systemd-vmspawn boot a session image in seconds and share /job/<ws> with the guest (virtiofs)? If not, the question is moot. Revisit when the sandbox moves to a root-orchestrator host.$ !6 ^2026-10-08T10:31 #01a11c91-d731-7001-ac3a-4f6c93ecd401
[ ] Don't reuse a half-built landing candidate $build_candidate returns early when the candidate dir exists (\[ -d "\$cand" ] && return 0), but it can stop partway through: a submodule on a detached HEAD stops it after clone_local, leaving later repos without their branch switch or refs/agent/land-base. The next agent-land reuses that partial candidate, merges into it, runs the gate on a stale tree (it passed), then advance() fails with 'fatal: ambiguous argument refs/agent/land-base' and blames the repo with 'moved since the candidate was built'. Seen 2026-10-08 on workspace 20261008-100832. Fix: build into a temp dir and rename it into place only when every repo is marked, or remove the candidate on any stop inside build_candidate. Also run the detached-HEAD check on every repo before cloning anything. Second, related trap (same day): the candidate starts each submodule at the root repo's pinned commit (switch -C <branch> resets the branch name there) and records that as land-base. So a real submodule branch that is ahead of the pin can never advance, and the message says it 'moved since the candidate was built' when it never matched. Either check 'real branch == pinned commit' up front, with a message saying to bump the pointer first, or start the candidate from each real branch's tip.$ !3 +bug ^2026-10-08T10:52 #01a11ca5-8ac6-75e1-a249-607283abe5d2
[ ] Find why pi sessions block subagents; allow them if spend stays visible $agents/session runs pi with --exclude-tools bg_run,bg_delegate,subagent; the reason isn't recorded (carried over from agents/loop in adc89ea). Claude sessions allow subagents and cap spend with --max-budget-usd. Likely reasons: background tools outlive a headless session, and pi's cap is computed from message_end usage events that may not include subagent costs. Check whether subagent usage reaches the session's JSON log; if it does, allow subagent (keep bg_* blocked) so workers can save tokens the way the orchestrator does. Sessions must never launch sandbox sessions themselves (agent-run from inside); that stays with an orchestrator on the host (2026-10-08, Darrion and the assistant).$ !5 ^2026-10-08T11:30 #01a11cc7-d14a-75c1-afca-968ade66ddbd
[ ] Replace podman with mkosi images and systemd sessions $Darrion, 2026-10-09: see whether podman can go entirely, so personal-os needn't carry it. Assessed from the desktop: about ten call sites (build 2, run 2, secret 3, volume 1, unshare 1, rmi 1); sessions already run as systemd units. Build: mkosi profiles in personal-os (sandbox-worker base proven, personal-os 963c931), project layers as BaseTrees= or sysext (sysext covers only /usr and /opt). Run: systemd-nspawn or systemd-run with RootImage= and private users. Secrets: systemd credentials. Volumes: bind mounts. Blocker for unprivileged use: systemd-nsresourced and systemd-mountfsd are disabled on the desktop (a host change, Darrion's call); on the dev pod, as root, none. DECIDED 2026-10-09 by Darrion (answered first; my take agreed): support unprivileged systemd sandboxes; enable systemd-nsresourced.socket and systemd-mountfsd.socket on the desktop (Darrion runs it, needs root). To verify: mountfsd may require images verity-signed with a host-trusted key for unprivileged mounts, which ties to signing-key custody; directory roots should need only nsresourced. RESULT 2026-10-09 (tested on the desktop, systemd 262, mkosi 27.1): unprivileged nspawn cannot bind a user-owned clone (mountfsd idmaps only foreign-UID-range trees, by design), and a user unit with RootDirectory= shares the host network (IPAddressDeny= is ignored in user units; the session reached a service on the host's loopback). Decided with Darrion: a static system template, system/usr/lib/systemd/system/agent@.service, started by the agents group through a polkit rule; it runs as its own agent system user, and everything per run is data in /var/lib/agent-runs/<workspace> (root link, run.env, agents/, work/, home/, layers/). Verified in a real session: credential from /etc/credstore, read-only root, loopback and LAN blocked, DNS through the resolved stub, PID namespace. Layers: ExtensionDirectories= slots layers/harness and layers/project; the release file must be named after the slot (a mismatch is skipped silently under the - prefix). layers/harness (mkosi, Overlay=yes on a base tree) ran claude and pi in a session. Needs systemd >= 257. Dependencies point toward the base: the base OS knows no project, so this repo ships its units and layers and hosts install them. Hosts: on a plain Arch host, system/PKGBUILD (local makepkg -si; decided with Darrion, the proper Arch way; pacman's systemd hooks do sysusers, tmpfiles, daemon-reload); installed on the desktop 2026-10-09 and a session passed every check from the packaged unit. On personal-os hosts, a sysext later (it makes /usr read-only while merged, which blocks pacman on a mutable desktop). Gaps found 2026-10-09 while moving platform's toolchain to a project layer: (1) a project's Containerfile ENV has no layer equivalent, so .sandbox/setup exports it (e.g. CARGO_TARGET_DIR); (2) layers are read-only, so caches written at runtime (platform's /opt/uv-cache) become .sandbox/volumes mounts; (3) a project layer must install from the base's Arch snapshot, so the base should record its snapshot for agent-run to pass as --snapshot, not each project pinning its own; (4) agent-land's gate runs in podman and becomes an agent@ session too; (5) agent-run must decide when a layer is stale (podman's layer cache did this). Proven 2026-10-09: platform's full landing gate (.sandbox/setup, then scripts/validate-pinned) passed in an agent@ session on base + harness + project layers, exit 0 in 108 s. Gap (2) is not a gap: uv falls back to a cache in the writable home. Gap (6) found: git refuses a clone owned by another user; solved with GIT_CONFIG_* in the unit. polkit 127 takes the caller's process groups, so agents membership needs a fresh login (until then, newgrp agents). Gap (7), found 2026-10-09 converting layers to sysext images: an extension image carries only /usr and /opt, but Arch packages keep config in /etc (Arch's JDK: /usr/lib/jvm/java-21-openjdk/conf -> /etc/java21-openjdk), so ROBOT fails with 'Error loading java.security file'. The same layer as a directory passed the gate; why ExtensionDirectories= differed is unconfirmed. Gap (7) solved, decided with Darrion (confext pairs): each layer ships <slot>.raw (sysext: /usr, /opt) and <slot>-etc.raw (confext: /etc). Recipe, proven 2026-10-09: mkosi builds the layer once as Format=directory with Overlay=yes on the base, its mkosi.extra carrying both release files (usr/lib/extension-release.d/extension-release.<slot>, etc/extension-release.d/extension-release.<slot>-etc, ID=_any); then systemd-repart cuts both images with mkosi's own sysext-unsigned and confext-unsigned definitions (7 s), run under unshare -r with TMPDIR on a disk (not the /tmp tmpfs, which has a per-user quota). Platform's full gate passed on base + harness.raw + project.raw + project-etc.raw (27 s warm, no password prompt). The build belongs in agent-run's layer step, with staleness; layers/harness still builds Format=sysext directly until then. Format decided (meta-analysis DECISIONS 6e43036): our images and layers are DDIs (sysext), OCI only for pulling others' images; layers move from directories to mkosi sysext images and ExtensionImages=. Next: then agent-run writes run directories instead of Quadlet units; this charter's Containerfile principle and dependency table change when that lands. Step B, 2026-10-09: the project layer is optional (31edc86); a real session in this repo ran on base + harness only. Remaining: agent-run builds layers itself (recipe above), with staleness; release files declare SYSEXT_LEVEL from the base instead of ID=_any.$ !3 ^2026-10-09T01:23 #01a11fc2-f801-70e1-9d36-1bad0f375602
[ ] Harden host git against agent-written repository config $Found 2026-10-09 auditing the systemd runtime: the agent can write work/.git/config (and hooks) in its clone, and some config keys run commands when the host runs git there (core.fsmonitor, core.hooksPath, filters on checkout). True under Podman too (the agent ran as the person). Every host git call on a workspace clone should neutralize repository config that executes (e.g. -c core.fsmonitor= -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null, no checkout of agent-controlled attributes), or fetch from the clone into a host-owned repository and work there. Needs a test that plants such config and proves the host never runs it.$ !2 ^2026-10-09T13:31 #01a1225d-1458-7389-919c-274df9e1d60d
